    <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6 faq-answer">
     <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">How do I verify an FCC ID on a Chinese USB charger?</h3>
     <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">Enter the grantee code (first 3-5 characters) and product code (remaining characters) at fcc.gov/oet/ea/fccid. The result should show the grantee company matching the supplier's business license, application type "Certification" or "DoC", and downloadable test reports and photos. If the ID does not resolve or the company name mismatches, the certification is fake. WOWOHCOOL FCC ID resolves in under 30 seconds. Request a live database screenshot with any supplier.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6 faq-answer">
     <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">What is the difference between the real CE mark and the "China Export" CE?</h3>
     <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">The real CE (Conformité Européenne) has letters C and E formed from equal-diameter circles with a gap between them roughly half the letter width. The "China Export" imitation has letters closer together with no gap and disproportionate widths. Print both at the same size and overlay for a definitive visual comparison. Only the real CE authorizes EU sale. The China Export mark has no regulatory meaning.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6 faq-answer">
     <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">Can I verify a UL certificate for free, or does it cost money?</h3>
     <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">Free. UL Product iQ at productiq.ul.com requires only a free account (email registration, no credit card, 60-second signup). Search by file number (E-number, format E123456) or company name. Every UL certification, listing, and category assignment is publicly searchable at no cost. Amazon and major retailers run this exact check on every seller upload.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6 faq-answer">
     <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">What documents should a real charger OEM supplier provide with their certification?</h3>
     <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">Four per certification: (1) test report from an ISO 17025-accredited lab (30-80 pages, not a single certificate page), (2) Declaration of Conformity signed by the manufacturer referencing specific directives (LVD 2014/35/EU, EMC 2014/30/EU, RoHS 2011/65/EU), (3) technical file (schematic, BOM, PCB layout, risk assessment), (4) production consistency evidence (IQC records, batch tracking). A single-page "certificate" without these is not sufficient for cross-border sale.</p>
    </div>
    
    
    
    <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6 faq-answer">
